=== 1. if as a FUNCTION: both arguments are evaluated first ===
  my_if(True, 42, boom()) -> ZeroDivisionError: this branch should never have run
  The else branch ran even though the condition was True.

=== 2. Python's own if: non-strict ===
  42 if True else boom() = 42

=== 3. The fix real languages use: pass THUNKS ===
  my_if_lazy(True, lambda: 42, lambda: boom()) = 42
